## Finding 1: `hcm-c` is emitted with the runtime that has no raw file I/O

`hcm-read-raw` / `hcm-write-raw` / `hcm-enc-file` call `w-sys #904` (file size), `#902` (raw read),
`#903` (raw write) and `#900`/`#901` (mark/rewind). `emit-c-cli` (lib/native) passes these to the
kernel's `syscall()`, which answers ENOSYS (-38). `fsz` becomes 2^64 − 38; `hcm-enc-file` then
computes `%wmul n #2`, which the runtime's checked `hy_mul` refuses: abort, no output, on every input.
`emit-c-cli-opt` (lib/native-opt, `ce-opt-posix-sys`) implements 900–904. So `hcm-c` is the wrong
emitter for this module; `emit-c-cli-opt (code hcm-main)` is the one that runs.

## Finding 2: `hy_rewind` frees the slab that holds the live continuation

With `emit-c-cli-opt`, inputs up to 654,589 B of `dickens` round-trip; from about 655,000 B the
encoder segfaults in `hy_app` (heap address, not stack). `hy_rewind` (#901, called every 256 bytes)
frees every slab after the marked one, and the continuation `k` of the #901 command itself was
allocated after the mark; when it sits in a later slab, `hy_run_step` applies a freed closure. A
`hy_rewind` that resets `hy_cur/hy_at/hy_end` to the mark and keeps the later slabs (which
`hy_alloc` already reuses through `hy_cur->next`) removes the fault: 2,000,000 B of `dickens` and
all of `xml` then encode.

## Finding 3: the encoder's stack grows with the input

The per-byte loop (`hcm-enc-loop2` and its helpers) is emitted as direct calls between functions of
15–24 arguments. gcc and clang do not make these sibling calls on x86-64 (6 argument registers), so
every byte nests frames: 484 MB of stack after 3 s on a 2 MB input (gcc), 721 MB (clang). With the
stack unlimited, `xml` encodes and its decode is killed for memory; `dickens` (10 MB) encode is
killed for memory. On ARM64 the register budget is 8, still below these arities. A loop emitted for
a self tail call, or the loop state packed into ≤ 6 (x86-64) / ≤ 8 (ARM64) words as the module's
header intends ("<= 8 args in x0..x7"), makes the stack constant.

## Measured (round trip bit-exact, `cmp` equal), bits per byte

| input | hx cm | xz -9e | bzip2 -9 | gzip -9 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| dickens, first 100,000 B | 2.5597 | 2.8544 | 2.6298 | 3.1166 |
| dickens, first 2,000,000 B | 2.2198 | 2.3469 | 2.2204 | 3.0278 |
| xml, first 2,000,000 B | 0.7936 | 0.8629 | 0.7704 | — |
| xml, whole (encode only; decode killed for memory) | 0.6639 | 0.6508 | 0.6602 | — |

Encode speed: 2,000,000 B in 5.5 s (about 0.36 MB/s). zstd is not installed on this machine.

## Where the README's 1.6305 bits/byte comes from

No module holds the number or the corpus it was measured on. Measured here on text (dickens), hx cm
matches bzip2 -9 and beats xz -9e; on xml it is between them. The comparison that would place it is
the whole of Silesia and enwik8 against zstd -19, xz -9e, and a context-mixing coder (paq8/cmix),
which needs Findings 1–3 resolved to run at full size.
